  "notes": "West leads the ♥4 against 3♦. South should play the ♥J at trick 1. Not only is East almost certain to hold the ♥A, even if West had that card he would not underlead it. It is quite possible, however, that West has the ♥Q.\n\nEast wins the ♥Q and cashes the ♥A. East switches to spades and South wins in dummy. There is no reason to put off drawing trump. West wins the ♦A and continues spades, dummy winning.\n\nAfter drawing trump, South must avoid losing 2 club tricks. When South leads a club and West follows low, South has a decision to make. If West has the ♣A and East has the ♣Q, it is necessary to play the ♣K from dummy.\n\nThe above distribution is unlikely, however, given the bidding. The above East hand has only 11 high card points, generally not enough for an opening bid. West's hand is also inconsistent with the bidding. Holding 2 aces, a doubleton, and trump support, West would have supported hearts instead of passing.\n\nThe above lie of the cards is much more likely. East has a full 13 point opener and West has only 6 points. If South plays the ♣K, East will win the ♣A and West will still get a trick with the ♣Q.\n\nTherefore, South should not play the ♣K on the first round. Since dummy's lower clubs are solid, playing any one of them will do. East wins the ♣A and South makes the contract.",
